Run the benchmark on real data: two engines fail outright

tools/bench_engines.py gains --real, which benchmarks an actual Parquet
file instead of generated arithmetic, and a set of real_operations
chosen to be the shapes that break systems rather than the ones that
flatter them: a skewed range, a conjunction, a disjunction, a low-
cardinality group, a high-cardinality group on a measure (not a
category), a date-part group, an inner join to a small dimension table,
and a left join.

The synthetic data exists to be predictable, which is precisely what makes
it unrepresentative. Here is the answer to "does this survive real
pipelines": on 1,000,000 rows of real NYC taxi data, two of four
executing engines FAIL.

  group_by_datepart        arrow   FAIL  SchemaDriftError: fare_amount:
  group_by_datepart        pandas  FAIL    nulls appeared in a non-nullable
                                          column
  group_by_datepart        duckdb        4,152.56 ms
  group_by_datepart        polars_cpu      14.21 ms

  group_by_cardinality     arrow   FAIL  same SchemaDriftError
  group_by_cardinality     pandas  FAIL  same SchemaDriftError

  join                     arrow   FAIL  same SchemaDriftError
  join                     pandas  FAIL  same SchemaDriftError
  join                     duckdb    1,195.87 ms
  join                     polars_cpu   76.54 ms

Everything that completed agrees exactly, on real data, across engines -
that part of the correctness claim holds. The failures are all the same
one, and it is the most important thing in this commit.

THE CAUSE. arrow_to_canonical infers nullability from the Arrow schema. A
Parquet file that contains nulls frequently does not record that fact in
its schema, so the canonical Schema is built saying "non-nullable", and
the executor's null handling then raises SchemaDriftError when it meets
the first null. Grouping a column with a million distinct values
materialises far more of it; joining touches all of it; either one
surfaces the mismatch that a small aggregate happened to avoid.

Why the synthetic benchmark could never have found it: the generated data
has no nulls, so the bug is invisible until real data with real nulls
arrives. This is the strongest argument yet for testing on real files.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A SLOWDOWN. arrow and pandas are exactly the
engines the air-gapped story depends on - arrow is the fallback that runs
when nothing else is installed, and a `pip install aar[arrow]` install has
no alternative. An engine that cannot group or join a real Parquet file
without raising is not a fallback, it is a failure with a worse error
message.

Also visible, and not a defect: duckdb's limit takes 84 ms against
polars' 0.97 ms. Relation materialisation for a 1,000-row head is pure
overhead on a column engine. It is in the outlier list because the ratio
is real, not because anything is wrong.

NOT FIXED HERE. The fix is to stop inferring nullability from schema
metadata and to take it from the data, which means a null-count pass on
load - a real design change to the interchange layer, not a patch. It
needs its own commit with its own tests, and the honest version of this
report is that AAR currently does NOT survive real data on the two
engines an air-gapped install depends on.
